  "response": "I'd strengthen **`crime_00009`** first. It's the only finding with no assertions at all that tests an esbuild plugin.\n\n## The finding\n\n`test/index.test.ts:537-548`, claim `no_assertions`, severity `medium`, confidence `0.88`. The evidence, quoted:\n\n> - `\"test: \\\"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\\\"\"`\n> - `\"0 expect/assert calls\"`\n> - `\"lines 537-548\"`\n> - `\"blast radius scored by the code under test: src/utils.ts\"`\n\nIts summary: *\"contains no expect/assert calls. A test that only runs setup gives agents false confidence.\"*\n\n## Why this one explains the escaped regression\n\nThe test calls `run()` and never reads what it returns:\n\n```ts\ntest('native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly', async () => {\n  await run(getTestName(), {\n    'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n    'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n  }, { entry: ['input.tsx'] })\n})\n```\n\nIt only fails if the build throws. That matters because of how `src/esbuild/native-node-module.ts` works. The plugin catches `./hi.node` with an `onResolve` filter of `/\\.node$/`, then checks `resolvedId.endsWith('.node')`:\n\n- A real `.node` file goes to the `node-file` namespace.\n- `hi.node.js` should fall through to a plain `{ path: resolvedId }` and be bundled inline.\n\nThat fall-through branch is what this test exists to protect. Say a regression sends `.node.js` files into the `node-file` namespace by mistake. The build still succeeds, because the `node-file` loader wraps the `require(path)` in `try { … } catch {}` and swallows the failure. Then `hi` silently disappears from the bundle, and the test stays green.\n\nThe other no-assertion tests (`crime_00012`, `00013`, `00014`, `00028`, `00029`) are about `--dts`, `--dts-resolve` and tsconfig, not esbuild plugins.\n\n## The assertion I'd add\n\n`run()` in `test/utils.ts` returns `output` (the contents of `dist/input.js`) and `outFiles`. The test just above it uses both, so I'd follow the same pattern:\n\n```ts\n  const { output, outFiles } = await run(\n    getTestName(),\n    {\n      'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n      'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n    },\n    { entry: ['input.tsx'] },\n  )\n  // hi.node.js must be bundled inline, not routed through the node-file namespace\n  expect(output).toMatch(/hi\\s*=\\s*[\"']hi[\"']/)\n  expect(output).not.toMatch(/require\\([^)]*hi\\.node/)\n  expect(outFiles).toEqual(['input.js'])\n```\n\nEach line catches a different way it can break:\n\n- **The `toMatch` line** checks that the value from `hi.node.js` actually made it into the bundle.\n- **The `not.toMatch` line** fails if the file was wrongly treated as a native addon, because the bundle would then contain a `require(...hi.node...)` call.\n- **The `outFiles` line** fails if a stray `.node` file is emitted through the `file` loader.\n\nYou could also add `expect(output).toMatchSnapshot()` to match the neighbouring tests. I'd keep the explicit checks anyway: a snapshot regenerated with `-u` would happily accept the broken output.\n\nI haven't run this, so the regex may need adjusting to esbuild's exact output. Before merging, break the plugin on purpose (for example, remove the `endsWith('.node')` check) and confirm the test goes red.\n\n## Next in line\n\n`crime_00018`, the `\"external\"` test at `test/index.test.ts:133-156`, also covers an esbuild plugin (`src/esbuild/external.ts`). It's flagged `weak_assertion_matchers` with evidence `\"1 weak assertion matcher\"`. It's lower priority because it asserts something, but it's the next place a plugin regression could slip through.",
